The raw ndc is genuinely `null` for A/D at (±2,0). That means `v.project(camera)` is returning a vector with NaN/null components. This is the degenerate side-on case: a point at (±2, 0.5, 0) in world — wait, logical (±2,0) → world (±2, 0.5, -0). The camera is at (0,1.7,0) looking toward -Z. A point at world x=±2, z=0 is directly to the side, in the camera's local plane where the view-space z (depth) is 0 → division by zero in projection → NaN.

So `project()` legitimately gives NaN for points exactly in the near-plane (depth≈0). My earlier "horiz: left/right" was a bug in my own logic (NaN<0 is false, NaN>0 is false, so it fell to... actually it returned 'left' for A — that's wrong, must have been stale). Let me not rely on that.

The correct approach: A/D are pure lateral directions at the player's own position, so they're always in the near-plane and can't be projected. Instead, I should prove A/D by projecting a point that is *behind the camera's facing but offset sideways* — i.e., combine with forward. But the task explicitly wants A and D alone proven as "visibly left" / "visibly right."

The robust way: project a point at logical (±1, -0.5) — slightly *behind* (so it's in front of the camera's back, giving a valid depth) — no. Better: the standard fix is to project a point offset slightly toward the camera so depth > 0. Let me project (±1, 0) but at a height and pull it slightly in +Z world (toward camera) so it's not exactly in the near plane. Actually the cleanest: project the *movement direction* by looking at where a point at (±1, 0) would appear relative to screen centre when it has any depth. 

Let me instead verify A/D using the camera's right vector directly (screen-space): the camera's world right vector projected tells us screen-left/right. But RULE ZERO wants screen projection. 

The truly correct screen-space proof for pure strafe: a point exactly lateral has zero depth, so it projects to infinity (off-screen) — that's mathematically expected and not a defect. To make it projectable, give it a tiny depth by moving it a hair toward the camera (world +Z by 0.01). Let me try logical (±1, -0.01) → world (±1, 0.5, +0.01), which is 1cm in front of the near plane.
